  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1030 PM MDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nFlow aloft is beginning to shift from westerly to southwesterly as \nan area of low pressure descends along the West Coast. This is \nbeginning to pull high-level moisture into the Southwest, showing up \nas increasing upper level clouds. This trend of increasing upper \nlevel moisture will continue through the day Thursday and into \nFriday, though the lower levels will remain dry. A wave rotating \naround the parent low, still stationed off the Pacific Northwest \ncoast, will provide lift to kick off showers that linger through the \nday Saturday. PWAT values by this point are projected to be over \n200% of normal, although dry surface levels will take time to \nsaturate. Precipitation through the day on Saturday will be on the \nlighter side as a result, with predominately rain showers, with some \nsnow possible above 10k feet. A stray thunderstorm or two will be \npossible as well, which could produce brief heavier rainfall across \nthe region.\n\nA stronger moisture push associated with an atmospheric river (AR) \narrives on Sunday. This AR is expected to take a southern track \nthrough Baja California and into the Desert Southwest. There remains \na bit of disagreement between model solutions as to just how far \nsouth this moisture plume tracks. The ECMWF Ensemble tracks the \nplume further north, and has higher anomalies, with PWATs expected \nto be above 300% of normal near the Four Corners on Sunday. The \nEnsemble GFS is further south and has lower anomalies, with PWATs \ncloser to 250% of normal during the same period. Either way, this is \nan impressive amount of moisture that, when combined with enhanced \nlift ahead of the approaching low, will result in widespread \nprecipitation. Models do still agree on an area of 1-2 inches of \nrainfall across southwest Colorado and southeast Utah Sunday into \nMonday. River flooding remains unlikely at this point, as these \ntotals will be spread over an almost 48 hour period. What is a \nbigger concern is flash flooding across areas with still saturated \nsoils from last week's rainfall, particularly for areas of steep \nterrain, and over recent burn scars. A cold front pushes through \nfrom northwest to southeast Sunday night into Monday, with snow \nlevels dropping to around 8000 feet by Monday morning, and close to \n7000 feet by the evening as colder air pushes in behind the front. \nThis will bring snow, which could be intense at times, down to pass \nlevel. Mountain passes will likely see some travel slowdowns for \nmotorists Monday morning and into the afternoon. Precipitation will \ntaper off Monday night, with colder and drier conditions anticipated \nfor Tuesday and beyond.\n\nTemperatures will steadily cool Thursday onward through the weekend, \nas clouds and showers overspread the area. Behind the cold front, \nhighs on Monday and Tuesday will run close to 10 degrees below \nnormal, and area expected to remain in this range through much of \nthe rest of the week.",
